{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790979172,"nonce":"uQwgHFdRYzguNYMn/K3SN87dzs4=","title":"Sys.systemName() is empty in a wasm build","message":"On wasm32-wasip1-threads, `Sys.systemName()` returns \"\" (length 0), under `ash run` and in Ash's page, with and without ASH_WASM_FIBERS. Natively the same bytecode prints \"Mac\".\n\n    class SysName {\n        static function main() {\n            var n = Sys.systemName();\n            Sys.println('systemName \"' + n + '\" length ' + n.length);\n        }\n    }\n\n    haxe -main SysName -hl sysname.hl\n    ash sysname.hl                                                    # \"Mac\" length 3\n    ash --build out/sysname.wasm --target wasm32-wasip1-threads sysname.hl\n    ash run out/sysname.wasm                                          # \"\" length 0\n\nstd/src/sys.rs's hlp_sys_string falls through to std::env::consts::OS, which is \"wasi\" on that target, so the empty string comes from somewhere else in the wasm path: the native the AOT build binds, or the UCS-2 allocation.\n\nA program cannot use it to tell a page from a native run. hlwindow's GPU example takes a --poll flag instead.\n\nFound with ash 5aa28fd (local, two files modified) on 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}